{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"types-variables","title":"Fundamental Types & Variables","group":"Language Basics","order":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every variable has a fixed type chosen at compile time. The type decides its size, its range and which operations are legal.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Built-in types</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Typical size (64-bit)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td>A character <em>and</em> a small integer; signedness is platform-defined</td></tr><tr><td><code>short</code></td><td>2 bytes</td><td>At least 16 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td>At least 16 bits — in practice 32</td></tr><tr><td><code>long</code></td><td>8 bytes (Linux/macOS), 4 (Windows)</td><td>At least 32 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>long long</code></td><td>8 bytes</td><td>At least 64 bits — use for big sums</td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td>~7 significant digits</td></tr><tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>8 bytes</td><td>~15–16 significant digits — the default for decimals</td></tr><tr><td><code>long double</code></td><td>8–16 bytes</td><td>Extended precision where supported</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::size_t</code></td><td>8 bytes</td><td>Unsigned; the type of <code>sizeof</code> and container sizes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;climits&gt;\n#include &lt;cstdint&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;limits&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(int) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(long long) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(double) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; INT_MAX &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::numeric_limits&lt;int&gt;::min() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::numeric_limits&lt;long long&gt;::max() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::int32_t a = 1;          // exact-width types from &lt;cstdint&gt;\n    std::uint64_t b = 1;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(a) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(b) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4 8 8\n2147483647 -2147483648\n9223372036854775807\n4 8</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Sizes of <code>int</code>, <code>long</code> and friends are only <em>minimums</em> in the standard. When the exact width matters (file formats, hashing, bit tricks), use <code>std::int32_t</code>, <code>std::uint64_t</code>, … from <code>&lt;cstdint&gt;</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Ways to initialise</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;        // copy initialisation\n    int b(5);         // direct initialisation\n    int c{5};         // brace (uniform) initialisation — C++11\n    int d{};          // value-initialised → 0\n    int e = {5};      // copy-list initialisation\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; d &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>55505</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Brace initialisation <strong>refuses narrowing conversions</strong> — a lossy conversion becomes a compile error instead of a silent bug:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>int main() {\n    double price = 9.99;\n    int cents{price};     // error: narrowing conversion from double to int\n    return cents;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Compiler error</figcaption><pre><code>error: type 'double' cannot be narrowed to 'int' in initializer list [-Wc++11-narrowing]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A local variable of built-in type that is not initialised holds an <em>indeterminate</em> value — reading it is undefined behaviour. Always initialise: <code>int count = 0;</code> or <code>int count{};</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Integer overflow &amp; unsigned wrap-around</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;climits&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    unsigned int u = 0;\n    u = u - 1;                        // unsigned arithmetic wraps modulo 2^32\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; u &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    long long big = 1LL * INT_MAX * 2; // widen *before* multiplying\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; big &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    int s = -1;\n    unsigned int one = 1;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (s &lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(one)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // compare as signed\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4294967295\n4294967294\n1</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — signed overflow (UBSan aborts)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;climits&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int x = INT_MAX;\n    x = x + 1;                // signed overflow: undefined behaviour\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>UBSan</figcaption><pre><code>runtime error: signed integer overflow: 2147483647 + 1 cannot be represented in type 'int'</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><em>Unsigned</em> overflow is well-defined (wraps). <em>Signed</em> overflow is <strong>undefined behaviour</strong> — the optimiser may assume it never happens. Compute <code>a * b</code> in <code>long long</code> when it can exceed ~2·10⁹.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number\"></i>Literals</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Literal</th><th>Type / meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>42</code>, <code>42u</code>, <code>42L</code>, <code>42LL</code>, <code>42ULL</code></td><td><code>int</code>, <code>unsigned</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>long long</code>, <code>unsigned long long</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>0x2A</code>, <code>052</code>, <code>0b101010</code></td><td>Hex, octal, binary (C++14) — all 42</td></tr><tr><td><code>1'000'000</code></td><td>Digit separators (C++14)</td></tr><tr><td><code>3.14</code>, <code>3.14f</code>, <code>1e9</code></td><td><code>double</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'a'</code>, <code>\"abc\"</code></td><td><code>char</code>, <code>const char[4]</code> (array incl. <code>'\\0'</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"abc\"s</code>, <code>\"abc\"sv</code></td><td><code>std::string</code>, <code>std::string_view</code> (with <code>using namespace std::literals</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nullptr</code></td><td>The null pointer literal (type <code>std::nullptr_t</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-decimal\"></i>Floating point is approximate</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cmath&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    double a = 0.1 + 0.2;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (a == 0.3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';                   // 0: not exactly equal\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (std::fabs(a - 0.3) &lt; 1e-9) &lt;&lt; '\\n';  // 1: compare with a tolerance\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; 7 / 2 &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; 7 / 2.0 &lt;&lt; '\\n';      // integer vs floating division\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0\n1\n3 3.5</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between int x; and int x{}; inside a function?</summary><div><p><code>int x;</code> leaves <code>x</code> uninitialised — reading it is undefined behaviour. <code>int x{};</code> <em>value-initialises</em> it to <code>0</code>. For class types with a default constructor both call that constructor.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer brace initialisation?</summary><div><p>It works the same way for every type (built-ins, aggregates, classes, containers), it rejects narrowing conversions, and it avoids the <em>most vexing parse</em> (<code>Widget w();</code> declares a function, <code>Widget w{};</code> creates an object). The one gotcha: for types with an <code>initializer_list</code> constructor, <code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{3, 0}</code> means the elements <code>{3, 0}</code>, not “3 zeros”.</p></div></details></section>"}